    "q-138534": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138534",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
