      "source_example_id": "e-9830",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition",
      "kp_id": "e-9830"
    },
    "q-138669": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138669",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
