    "q-138621": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138621",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
